Placenta Praevia
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Painless vaginal bleeding, Soft non-tender uterus, Placenta covering cervical os on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-032
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of sudden, painless, bright red vaginal bleeding at 32 weeks gestation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and husband
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated sudden, moderate-volume bright red vaginal bleeding without pain in a third-trimester pregnancy. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to immediately assess maternal hemodynamic stability, confirm fetal wellbeing, and initiate emergency ambulance transport to a tertiary obstetric unit to manage potential life-threatening hemorrhage before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am 32 weeks pregnant, and I just stood up from the couch and suddenly felt a gush of bright red blood, but I have absolutely no pain.
History of Present Illness: The patient, currently at 32 weeks gestation, reports a sudden onset of painless bright red vaginal bleeding occurring 30 minutes ago. She describes the volume as approximately half a cup of fresh blood, which soaked through her clothing. She explicitly notes the absence of significant abdominal pain, cramping, or uterine tenderness. The bleeding was unprovoked tonight, though she reports a history of minor spotting provoked by sexual intercourse two weeks ago. She has a history of one prior cesarean section. She reports the baby is moving normally.
Functional Status: Strictly resting on the couch, terrified to move.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of catastrophic maternal hemorrhage and fetal hypoxia; husband is highly anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, anxious, and is lying perfectly still on her left side.
Physical Exam: Abdominal palpation reveals a soft, non-tender, and relaxed uterus. There is no evidence of hypertonus, rigidity, or focal tenderness. Fundal height is consistent with dates. The presenting fetal part feels high, and the lie appears transverse. Fetal heart rate, auscultated via handheld Doppler, is 140 bpm with a regular rhythm, indicating an absence of acute fetal distress. Speculum and bimanual examinations are STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED and were deferred to prevent catastrophic hemorrhage. Visual inspection of the patient's undergarments confirms a moderate volume of fresh, bright red blood.
Environmental Observations: Clear path for stretcher access.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking prenatal vitamins and iron supplements.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Placenta Praevia (Antepartum Hemorrhage) - Status: Active bleeding, currently hemodynamically stable
Differentials:

Placental Abruption - Ruled out clinically by the complete absence of abdominal pain, the soft, non-tender resting uterus, and the lack of dark, clotted blood typical of concealed abruption.

Vasa Praevia - Possible, but usually presents with fetal bradycardia/distress immediately upon bleeding (as the blood is fetal in origin); the normal FHR makes this less likely, but urgent ultrasound is still required.

Cervical Ectropion/Polyp - Unlikely to produce this sudden, moderate volume of bright red blood, though cannot be definitively ruled out without a speculum exam (which is contraindicated here).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Immediate threat to maternal and fetal life if bleeding accelerates)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the hallmark clinical picture of placenta praevia: sudden, painless, bright red vaginal bleeding in the third trimester, accompanied by a soft, non-tender uterus. The objective confirmation of normal fetal heart tones and maternal hemodynamic stability is reassuring, but the potential for rapid, catastrophic hemorrhage remains high. Urgent after-hours attendance was critical to assess stability, strictly enforce the contraindication of a digital pelvic exam (which could fataly rupture the placenta), and immediately coordinate priority emergency transport to a tertiary maternity hospital equipped for emergency cesarean section and neonatal intensive care. This assessment and coordination could not be delayed.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Placed the patient in a strict left lateral decubitus position to optimize uteroplacental perfusion. Activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for priority 1 transport to the tertiary obstetric hospital. Secured a 16G peripheral IV cannula in the left cubital fossa while awaiting paramedics. Explicitly documented "NO VAGINAL EXAM" to pass to the receiving team.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent putting any pressure on the cervix, remain strictly lying on your left side and do not attempt to stand or walk to the bathroom under any circumstances; recognizing the absence of further gushing will enforce this absolute physical restriction. To assist the hospital team, remain entirely nil by mouth (no food or water); understanding that you may require an emergency cesarean section tonight will ensure strict compliance with fasting.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated and on scene.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Direct clinical handover provided to the responding paramedic crew.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched for immediate emergency transport to the hospital due to high risk of massive obstetric hemorrhage.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Painless bleeding in the third trimester requires immediate, mandatory hospital-based ultrasound and monitoring.
Clinical Course: The bleeding may temporarily stop or continue. The hospital will perform an ultrasound to locate the placenta and monitor the baby continuously.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not move from your left side. Do not eat or drink anything. Wait for the paramedics to move you via stretcher.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Based on your painless, bright red bleeding and your soft stomach, it is highly likely your placenta is sitting very low, covering the opening of your womb (placenta praevia). As your womb stretches, small blood vessels on the placenta tear. It is absolutely critical that nothing is inserted into your vagina, and you must go to the hospital immediately by ambulance so they can monitor you and the baby, and prepare for a safe delivery if the bleeding does not stop.
Actionable Steps: Lie completely still on your left side. Do not eat or drink. Let the paramedics handle all of your movement to the stretcher.

